                          %mo ⊂
                          %mi M
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B \subset M
                %li
                  %p
                    if we have some configuration of balls, like 2 balls
                    %math#mathml_a59c9603e2dda1396ed3a32aa551b8822ea52dce_180.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi B
                            %mn 1
                          %mo ,
                          %msub
                            %mi B
                            %mn 2
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B_1, B_2
                    inside a big one
                    %math#mathml_a59c9603e2dda1396ed3a32aa551b8822ea52dce_181.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi B
                            %mn 3
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B_3
                    we get a map
                    %math#mathml_a59c9603e2dda1396ed3a32aa551b8822ea52dce_182.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi F
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %msub
                            %mi B
                            %mn 1
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo ⊗
                          %mi F
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %msub
                            %mi B
                            %mn 2
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo →
                          %mi F
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %msub
                            %mi B
                            %mn 3
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F(B_1)\otimes F(B_2) \to F(B_3)
                %li
                  %p these must vary smoothly as the configuration of the balls varies
          %p
            This is an algebra over the embedded little disk
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/operad"} operad
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/multicategory"} multicategory
            with more than one object)
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                where the colors are
                %math#mathml_a59c9603e2dda1396ed3a32aa551b8822ea52dce_183.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi B
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi M
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B(M)
            %li
              %p
                %math#mathml_a59c9603e2dda1396ed3a32aa551b8822ea52dce_184.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
                \-ary operations are
                %math#mathml_a59c9603e2dda1396ed3a32aa551b8822ea52dce_185.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi B
                        %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi M
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B_n(M)
            %li
              %p with extra conditions such that the vector space we assign to each color forms a smooth vector bundle
              %ul
                %li and all operad maps are compatible with this
          %p notice that it happenss that for given in and out colors, there is at least one morphism in the operad.
          %ul
            %li
              %p there are several different reductions of this structure that are more familiar
            %li
              %p
                notion of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+bundle"} vector bundle
                comes in three natural flavors
              %ul
                %li
                  %p
                    1)
                    %math#mathml_a59c9603e2dda1396ed3a32aa551b8822ea52dce_186.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %msup
                            %mi C
                            %mn ∞
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^\infty
                %li
                  %p 2) holomorphic
                %li
                  %p 3) locally constant sheaves
            %li
              %p definition of factorization algebra can be modified to the case of 2) and 3)
          %p
            %strong def
            a locally constant factorization algebra is like a factorization algebra, except that instead of being a vector bundle
            %math#mathml_a59c9603e2dda1396ed3a32aa551b8822ea52dce_187.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            is a locally constant sheaf on
            %math#mathml_a59c9603e2dda1396ed3a32aa551b8822ea52dce_188.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi M
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B(M)
            of co
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/chain+complex"}> chain complex
            es and the structure maps of locally constant sheaves
          %blockquote
            %p question: cohomologically locally constant or really locally constant
          %p &gt; answer: I think cohomologically locally constant
          %p
            let
            %math#mathml_a59c9603e2dda1396ed3a32aa551b8822ea52dce_189.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            be a locally constant factorization algebra in
            = succeed "," do
              %math#mathml_a59c9603e2dda1396ed3a32aa551b8822ea52dce_190.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^n
            %math#mathml_a59c9603e2dda1396ed3a32aa551b8822ea52dce_191.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B(\mathbb{R}^n)
            is contractible
            %math#mathml_a59c9603e2dda1396ed3a32aa551b8822ea52dce_192.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            is quasi-isomorphic to a trivial sheaf with fiber
            %math#mathml_a59c9603e2dda1396ed3a32aa551b8822ea52dce_193.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            a cochain complex
          %p
            so then for instance the map
            %code.maruku-mathml V__{B_1} \otimes V_{B_2}  \to V_{B_3}
            depends only on homotopies of the configuration,
          %p
            so, a locally constant factorization algebra on
            %math#mathml_a59c9603e2dda1396ed3a32aa551b8822ea52dce_194.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V__\mathbb{R}^n
            is an
            = succeed "-" do
              %math#mathml_a59c9603e2dda1396ed3a32aa551b8822ea52dce_195.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi E
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E_n
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebra+over+an+operad"} algebra
          %p
            next specialization:
            %strong holomorphic factorization algebras
          %p
            Let
            %math#mathml_a59c9603e2dda1396ed3a32aa551b8822ea52dce_196.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Σ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
            be a Riemannian surface.
          %p
            We know what it means for a map from a complex manifold to
            %math#mathml_a59c9603e2dda1396ed3a32aa551b8822ea52dce_197.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi M
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B(M)
            to be holomorphic; so we can talk about holomorphic algebras on
            %math#mathml_a59c9603e2dda1396ed3a32aa551b8822ea52dce_198.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Σ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B(\Sigma)
          %p
            a homomorphic map
            %math#mathml_a59c9603e2dda1396ed3a32aa551b8822ea52dce_199.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                  %mo →
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi M
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U \to B(M)
            is a bundle
            %math#mathml_a59c9603e2dda1396ed3a32aa551b8822ea52dce_200.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                  %mo →
                  %mi U
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M \to U
            all of whose fibers are balls and a map
          .maruku-equation
            %math#mathml_a59c9603e2dda1396ed3a32aa551b8822ea52dce_201.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi M
                        %mtd
                          %mo →
                        %mtd
                          %mi Σ
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi U
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  M &amp;\to&amp; \Sigma
                  \\\
                  \\downarrow
                  \\\
                  U
                  }
          %p
            actually
            = succeed "," do
              %math#mathml_a59c9603e2dda1396ed3a32aa551b8822ea52dce_202.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi M
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B(M)
          %blockquote
            %p
              Andre Henriquez: but with this definition, won’t every map
              %math#mathml_a59c9603e2dda1396ed3a32aa551b8822ea52dce_203.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                    %mo →
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi M
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U \to B(M)
              be holomorphic: &gt; answer: oops, right
          %p ??
          %p
            let’s consider a holomorphic factorization algebra on
            = succeed "," do
              %math#mathml_a59c9603e2dda1396ed3a32aa551b8822ea52dce_204.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
          %p
            let
            %math#mathml_a59c9603e2dda1396ed3a32aa551b8822ea52dce_205.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                  %mo =
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V = F
            (any round disk)
          %p
            if we have a configuratoin of disks with
            %math#mathml_a59c9603e2dda1396ed3a32aa551b8822ea52dce_206.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi B
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B_1
            and
            %math#mathml_a59c9603e2dda1396ed3a32aa551b8822ea52dce_207.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi B
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B_2
            in
            %math#mathml_a59c9603e2dda1396ed3a32aa551b8822ea52dce_208.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi B
                    %mn 3
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B_3
            with radii
            %math#mathml_a59c9603e2dda1396ed3a32aa551b8822ea52dce_209.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi ϵ
                    %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \epsilon_i
            with one disk in the center of the big one and the other at complex parameter
            = succeed "," do
              %math#mathml_a59c9603e2dda1396ed3a32aa551b8822ea52dce_210.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi z
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} z
          .maruku-equation
            %math#mathml_a59c9603e2dda1396ed3a32aa551b8822ea52dce_211.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi m
                    %mi z
                  %mo :
                  %mi V
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi V
                  %mo →
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  m_z : V \otimes V \to V
          %p
            must vary holomorphically with
            %math#mathml_a59c9603e2dda1396ed3a32aa551b8822ea52dce_212.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi z
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} z
          %p
            so
            %math#mathml_a59c9603e2dda1396ed3a32aa551b8822ea52dce_213.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi z
                  %mo ↦
                  %msub
                    %mi m
                    %mi z
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} z \mapsto m_z
            is a holomorphic map
            = succeed "," do
              %math#mathml_a59c9603e2dda1396ed3a32aa551b8822ea52dce_214.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Annulus
                    %mo →
                    %mi Hom
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi V
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi V
                    %mo ,
                    %mi V
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Annulus \to Hom(V \otimes V, V)
          .maruku-equation
            %math#mathml_a59c9603e2dda1396ed3a32aa551b8822ea52dce_215.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi m
                    %mi z
                  %mo ∼
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                    %mrow
                      %mi k
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi ℤ
                  %msup
                    %mi Z
                    %mi k
                  %msub
                    %mi a
                    %mi k
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  m_z \sim \sum_{k \in \mathbb{Z}} Z^k a_k
          %p
            with
            %math#mathml_a59c9603e2dda1396ed3a32aa551b8822ea52dce_216.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi a
                    %mi k
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a_k
            in some completion of
            %math#mathml_a59c9603e2dda1396ed3a32aa551b8822ea52dce_217.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Hom
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi V
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi V
                  %mo ,
                  %mi V
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Hom(V \otimes V , V)
          %blockquote
            %p question by Ulrich Bunke: algebraic tensor product or not? &gt; answer: no, in examples tensor product will be projective tensor product
          %p reminiscent of vertex operator algebra
          %p notice that Beilinson-Drinfeld make the same def in the algebraic setting, in their case the axioms are equivalent to that of a vertex operator algebra;
          %p
            they show axioms for chiral algebra on
            %math#mathml_a59c9603e2dda1396ed3a32aa551b8822ea52dce_218.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
            are essentially equivalent to those of a vertex operator algebra
          %p
            %strong> claim
            \: structure of factorization algebra: good to encode quantum field theory
          %p notice that factorizations algebras on real line tend to be associativ algebras, so that fits in with the expectation from quantum mechanics.
          %hr/
          %p
            No previous day —
